Distributed preprocessing
=========================

Run a build under `pump` to distribute preprocessing as well as compilation:

    pump make -j"$(distcc -j)"

The build must invoke distcc as its compiler, as with ordinary distcc. Pump
starts one include-analysis process for the build and stops it when the command
exits. It does not require build-system hooks or special handling of generated
headers. Without pump, ordinary client-side preprocessing remains the default.
For Clang C-family sources, the client rewrites includes while preserving macros
and the worker finishes preprocessing. This preserves macro-sensitive diagnostics
without requiring application headers on workers. Compiler versions, targets
and default compiler configuration must still match. Dependency files are
produced on the client.

With DISTCC_BROKER set, clients request remote preprocessing through the existing
broker. Adaptive scheduling and the TCP connection pool are reused. These jobs
do not reserve a local preprocessing slot. Without a broker, configure remote
hosts with the `,cpp,lzo` options.

The include server conservatively analyzes include directives and caches both
the dependency graph and compressed source/header files. Before reusing the
graph it checks metadata for positive and negative include lookups, including
ancestor identities. Header creation, modification, removal and symlink
replacement invalidate the graph. A new generation holds new compressed files;
previously issued manifests keep their old files until pump exits. A change
detected while gathering inputs causes local preprocessing instead. Build
dependencies must still prevent writes to an input during its compilation.
An aborted analysis also invalidates the graph: incomplete nodes must not be
reused by later requests, including requests for the same translation unit.

The worker receives the raw source and needed application headers, runs the
compiler's preprocessor and compiles the result. System headers in the compiler's
default search paths are not transferred: compiler versions, target, search paths
and default system headers must match between client and workers. A cross compiler
must provide the same target headers. Pump does not synchronize a sysroot.

Standalone GCC and Clang `-E` calls with a source file are also distributed.
Standard output, `-o` output files, explicit `-MF` dependencies, exit status and
partial standard output on preprocessing errors are returned to the client.
The worker's temporary path is removed from preprocessor output. Standard output
is staged until the response finishes, so a dropped connection followed by a
local retry does not duplicate output. Upgrade clients and workers together to
use remote `-E`.

Stdin input, dependency-only `-M`/`-MM`, `-E -MD` without explicit `-MF`, absolute
include directives, computed `__has_include` operands and `#embed` remain local.
Literal `__has_include` probes participate in include lookup invalidation even
when no include directive follows. Other include-analysis failures fall back to
ordinary distcc; standalone preprocessing falls back to a local compiler when
DISTCC_FALLBACK permits it. DISTCC_TESTING_INCLUDE_SERVER=1 turns analysis fallback
into an error for validation.

DISTCC_CLIENT_TMP selects the compressed-input storage directory. Deep absolute
paths are supported when the variable is shared by pump and its distcc clients.
Storage for old generations is reclaimed at pump shutdown. Files are compressed
once per generation but still transferred per job; worker file caching is not
part of this protocol. Speedups depend on preprocessing cost, include-analysis
cost, network throughput and the number of independent build jobs.

`make preprocess-check` exercises real GCC/Clang preprocessing through distccd,
mutable and generated headers, dependency output, compiler errors, interrupted
responses, broker admission and pooled connections.
